module.exports=[84338,(a,b,c)=>{b.exports=[{id:2,title:"Support 4.0 GPA scale",kind:"feature",state:"open",labels:["good first issue"],opened_by:"curious-dev",opened_at:"2026-04-02",assigned_to:null,body:"It would be nice to support the 4.0 scale used by many international universities. The conversion table is out there so it should be easy to add a mode flag.\n\nWould be happy to contribute if this is welcome!",comments:[{author:"amaan-dev",date:"2026-04-02",body:"Good idea. Should be easy enough — just a second GRADE_POINTS dict and a `scale` parameter. Feel free to pick it up."},{author:"random-dev-42",date:"2026-04-20",body:"Which 4.0 mapping are you thinking? There are at least three different ones — the standard US one, the WES conversion, and the one my university uses. They're not the same."},{author:"infra-nerd-9",date:"2026-08-30",body:"+1, would use this."}],lesson:"good_first_issue_hard",lesson_feedback:"The maintainer called it easy, but the comments reveal the real problem: there is no single 4.0 scale. Without a decision on which mapping to use, you can't write the code. Starting here means you'd spend most of your time waiting for a design decision that hasn't been made.",reproduction:null},{id:3,title:"KeyError when using grade 'A+'",kind:"bug",state:"open",correctOutcome:"cant_reproduce",labels:["bug"],opened_by:"preethi-codes",opened_at:"2026-05-04",assigned_to:null,body:"Calling `semester_gpa([('A+', 3)])` raises a KeyError.\n\n```\nKeyError: 'A+'\n```\n\nLooked at GRADE_POINTS and A+ is just not there.\n\n**Steps to reproduce:**\n1. Import `semester_gpa` from `src/calculator.py`\n2. Call `semester_gpa([('A+', 3)])`\n3. Observe: KeyError: 'A+'\n\n**Python:** 3.12",comments:[{author:"meera-writes-code",date:"2026-05-06",body:"Reproduced. B+ has the same issue. Seems like only A, B, C, O, F are in the dict."},{author:"dev-rohan",date:"2026-05-18",body:"Same here. Going to put in a fix."}],lesson:"already_fixed_by_pr",lesson_feedback:"This issue is still open, but if you run the code today it works fine. PR #5 added 'A+' and 'B+' to GRADE_POINTS back in June — it just wasn't linked to this issue so the tracker was never updated. Always reproduce before you start: one line in the shell would have told you the bug is gone.",reproduction:{issueId:"gradewise-3",buggyVersion:"pre-PR#5",fixedVersion:"post-PR#5",reproductionSteps:[{instruction:"Open src/calculator.py and look at the GRADE_POINTS import."},{instruction:"Call semester_gpa([('A+', 3)])"},{instruction:"Observe the result."}],expectedResult:"semester_gpa returns 9.0",buggyBehavior:{output:"KeyError: 'A+' — 'A+' was missing from GRADE_POINTS before PR #5.",type:"crash",verifiedByTest:!0},fixedBehavior:{output:"9.0 — 'A+' is now in GRADE_POINTS (added by PR #5).",type:"success",verifiedByTest:!0},regressionTestId:"gradewise/keyerror-grade"}},{id:6,title:"cgpa() ignores credit weights — gives wrong result",kind:"bug",state:"open",correctOutcome:"reproduced",labels:["bug"],opened_by:"infra-nerd-9",opened_at:"2026-06-10",assigned_to:"lena-open-source",body:"The `cgpa()` function just averages the semester GPAs without weighting by credits. Example:\n\n- Semester 1: one 2-credit 'O' course → GPA 10.0\n- Semester 2: one 6-credit 'B' course → GPA 6.0\n\nCurrent output: 8.0. Expected: 7.0 (because sem 2 has 3x the credits).\n\n**Steps to reproduce:**\n1. Run `cgpa([[('O', 2)], [('B', 6)]])`\n2. Compare: got 8.0, expected 7.0\n\n**Python:** 3.12",comments:[{author:"amaan-dev",date:"2026-06-11",body:"Confirmed. The current implementation treats every semester equally regardless of credit load. It should weight each semester's contribution by its total credits."},{author:"lena-open-source",date:"2026-09-05",body:"I'd like to fix this. Is it okay if I take it?"},{author:"amaan-dev",date:"2026-09-07",body:"Assigned to you, go ahead. Please add a test with the exact example from the issue."}],lesson:"claimed_never_started",lesson_feedback:"The maintainer confirmed the bug and it's real. But read the last two comments: someone was assigned to this three weeks ago and hasn't opened a PR yet. Before you start, leave a comment asking if they're still on it — otherwise you might duplicate their work.",reproduction:{issueId:"gradewise-6",buggyVersion:"HEAD",fixedVersion:"fixed",reproductionSteps:[{instruction:"Open src/calculator.py and find the cgpa function."},{instruction:"Run cgpa([[('O', 2)], [('B', 6)]])"},{instruction:"Compare the output to the expected value."}],expectedResult:"cgpa returns 7.0 — weighted by credits: (2×10 + 6×6) / (2+6) = 7.0",buggyBehavior:{output:"8.0 — cgpa averages semester GPAs without weighting: (10.0 + 6.0) / 2 = 8.0",type:"wrong_output",verifiedByTest:!0},fixedBehavior:{output:"7.0 — credit-weighted average: (20 + 36) / 8 = 7.0",type:"success",verifiedByTest:!0},regressionTestId:"gradewise/cgpa-ignores-credits"}},{id:9,title:"CGPA looks wrong sometimes",kind:"bug",state:"open",correctOutcome:"not_enough_info",labels:["bug"],opened_by:"frustrated-user-77",opened_at:"2026-08-28",assigned_to:null,body:"I've been using this for a while and sometimes my CGPA comes out different from what my college portal shows. Not sure what's going on, maybe a bug?",comments:[{author:"amaan-dev",date:"2026-08-29",body:"Hi, thanks for the report. Could you share the exact input you're passing — the grade and credit values for each semester? Also which Python version are you on? Hard to investigate without a reproducible case."}],lesson:"no_reproduction_steps",lesson_feedback:"No inputs, no expected vs actual values, no Python version. The maintainer asked for more information and there's been no follow-up. You can't fix what you can't reproduce. Ask for the missing details — don't guess.",reproduction:null},{id:10,title:"semester_gpa([]) raises ZeroDivisionError instead of returning 0.0",kind:"bug",state:"open",correctOutcome:"reproduced",labels:["bug","good first issue"],opened_by:"dev-rohan",opened_at:"2026-09-01",assigned_to:null,body:"Calling `semester_gpa([])` with an empty list raises a ZeroDivisionError instead of returning 0.0.\n\n```\nZeroDivisionError: division by zero\n```\n\nAn empty semester makes sense if a student took a leave — it should just return 0.0.\n\n**Steps to reproduce:**\n1. Import `semester_gpa` from `src/calculator.py`\n2. Call `semester_gpa([])`\n3. Observe: ZeroDivisionError\n\n**Python:** 3.12",comments:[{author:"amaan-dev",date:"2026-09-02",body:"Confirmed. An empty semester should return 0.0 — add `if not courses: return 0.0` at the top of the function. PRs welcome, please include a test that calls `semester_gpa([])` and checks the return value."}],lesson:"genuine_good_pick",lesson_feedback:"Good pick. Clear steps, reproduces immediately, maintainer confirmed the fix and the expected behaviour. One guard at the top of the function. Nobody has claimed it. This is exactly the kind of issue to start with.",reproduction:{issueId:"gradewise-10",buggyVersion:"HEAD",fixedVersion:"fixed",reproductionSteps:[{instruction:"Open src/calculator.py and find semester_gpa."},{instruction:"Call semester_gpa([])"},{instruction:"Observe the exception."}],expectedResult:"semester_gpa([]) returns 0.0",buggyBehavior:{output:"ZeroDivisionError: division by zero — total_credits is 0 when the list is empty.",type:"crash",verifiedByTest:!0},fixedBehavior:{output:"0.0 — early return when courses list is empty.",type:"success",verifiedByTest:!0},regressionTestId:"gradewise/empty-semester-crash"}}]},13053,(a,b,c)=>{b.exports=JSON.parse('[{"id":12,"title":"Saving a note with an empty title silently creates a broken note","state":"open","labels":["bug","good first issue"],"opened_by":"preethi-codes","opened_at":"2026-08-14","assigned_to":null,"body":"Hi, I found a bug when saving notes.\\n\\nIf you call `save_note(title=\'\', body=\'some text\')`, it returns `{\\"status\\": \\"saved\\"}` with no error. The note gets saved with a blank title, which breaks the UI when you try to list notes later.\\n\\nI expected it to return a validation error like `{\\"error\\": \\"Title cannot be empty.\\"}` instead.\\n\\n**Steps to reproduce:**\\n1. Call `save_note(title=\'\', body=\'some content\')`\\n2. Call `list_notes()`\\n3. You\'ll see a note with an empty title in the list\\n\\n**Expected:** Validation error returned\\n**Actual:** Note saved with blank title, no error\\n\\n**Version:** 1.0.0\\n**Python:** 3.12","comments":[{"author":"tariq-dev","date":"2026-08-14","body":"Good catch. The `save_note` function is missing input validation entirely. Small fix — just needs a check after `strip()`. Should be straightforward for a first contribution."},{"author":"random-dev-42","date":"2026-08-15","body":"Same issue here. Also happens with whitespace-only titles like `\'   \'`."},{"author":"preethi-codes","date":"2026-08-15","body":"Yes, whitespace-only is the same bug — `strip()` returns `\'\'` and it still saves."}],"lesson":"genuine_good_pick","lesson_feedback":"Good pick. Clear steps, reproducible, small scope — one check after strip(). The maintainer confirmed it\'s a good first contribution and no one has claimed it yet.","reproduction":{"issueId":"notes-app-12","buggyVersion":"1.0.0","fixedVersion":"1.1.0","reproductionSteps":[{"instruction":"Open notes.py and find the save_note function."},{"instruction":"Call save_note with an empty title: save_note(title=\'\', body=\'hello\')"},{"instruction":"Check the return value."},{"instruction":"Call list_notes() and inspect the saved notes."}],"expectedResult":"save_note returns {\\"error\\": \\"Title cannot be empty.\\"}","buggyBehavior":{"output":"Returns {\\"status\\": \\"saved\\", \\"id\\": 1} — note saved with blank title, no error.","type":"silent_failure","verifiedByTest":true},"fixedBehavior":{"output":"Returns {\\"error\\": \\"Title cannot be empty.\\"} — note is not saved.","type":"validation_error","verifiedByTest":true},"regressionTestId":"notes-app/empty-title-crash"}},{"id":7,"title":"KeyError crash when deleting a note that doesn\'t exist","state":"open","labels":["bug","good first issue"],"opened_by":"dev-rohan","opened_at":"2026-07-02","assigned_to":null,"body":"When I try to delete a note ID that doesn\'t exist (or was already deleted), the app throws a `KeyError` instead of returning a proper error message.\\n\\n```\\nKeyError: 99\\n```\\n\\nThis crashes the whole program if you\'re not catching exceptions. Expected it to return something like `{\\"error\\": \\"Note not found.\\"}` instead.\\n\\n**Steps to reproduce:**\\n1. Call `delete_note(99)` when no note with id 99 exists\\n2. Observe: unhandled KeyError\\n\\n**Version:** 1.0.0","comments":[{"author":"tariq-dev","date":"2026-07-03","body":"Confirmed. The function uses `del _notes[note_id]` directly without checking first. Thanks for the report."},{"author":"anon-contributor","date":"2026-07-10","body":"I\'ll take this one!"},{"author":"tariq-dev","date":"2026-07-11","body":"Sounds good @anon-contributor, go ahead."},{"author":"some-lurker","date":"2026-07-18","body":"any update on this? still broken for me"}],"lesson":"claimed_never_started","lesson_feedback":"Someone claimed this 4 months ago and the maintainer assigned it to them. No PR was ever opened. Before you start, leave a comment asking if it\'s still being worked on — otherwise you might duplicate effort or step on someone\'s toes.","reproduction":{"issueId":"notes-app-7","buggyVersion":"1.0.0","fixedVersion":"1.1.0","reproductionSteps":[{"instruction":"Open notes.py and find the delete_note function."},{"instruction":"Call delete_note(999) without creating any note with that ID first."},{"instruction":"Observe the exception raised."}],"expectedResult":"delete_note returns {\\"error\\": \\"Note with id 999 does not exist.\\"}","buggyBehavior":{"output":"Raises KeyError: 999 — unhandled exception, crashes the program.","type":"crash","verifiedByTest":true},"fixedBehavior":{"output":"Returns {\\"error\\": \\"Note with id 999 does not exist.\\"} — clean error, no crash.","type":"validation_error","verifiedByTest":true},"regressionTestId":"notes-app/delete-keyerror"}},{"id":15,"title":"search_notes does not find notes when query case doesn\'t match","state":"open","labels":["bug"],"opened_by":"meera-writes-code","opened_at":"2026-07-20","assigned_to":null,"body":"If I save a note titled \'Shopping List\' and then search for \'shopping\', I get an empty result. The search only works if the query matches the exact capitalisation of the title.\\n\\n```python\\nsave_note(title=\'Shopping List\')\\nresults = search_notes(\'shopping\')  # returns []\\nresults = search_notes(\'Shopping\')  # returns the note\\n```\\n\\nExpected search to be case-insensitive.\\n\\n**Version:** 1.0.0","comments":[{"author":"tariq-dev","date":"2026-07-21","body":"Classic one. The fix is `.lower()` on both sides of the comparison."},{"author":"meera-writes-code","date":"2026-07-21","body":"Yes, I figured it was something like that. Happy to submit a fix if no one else is on it."},{"author":"tariq-dev","date":"2026-07-22","body":"Someone\'s already working on it in a branch. Will update when merged."}],"lesson":"already_fixed_by_pr","lesson_feedback":"This issue is still marked open, but PR #38 merged last month and fixed exactly this bug. If you check the closed PRs, you\'ll see the fix is already in the codebase. Reproducing before you start would have caught this — the bug no longer exists in the current code.","reproduction":{"issueId":"notes-app-15","buggyVersion":"1.0.0","fixedVersion":"1.1.0","reproductionSteps":[{"instruction":"Open notes.py and find the search_notes function."},{"instruction":"Save a note: save_note(title=\'Shopping List\')"},{"instruction":"Search with lowercase: search_notes(\'shopping\')"},{"instruction":"Check the result."}],"expectedResult":"search_notes(\'shopping\') returns the note titled \'Shopping List\'","buggyBehavior":{"output":"Returns [] — case-sensitive match fails, note not found.","type":"wrong_output","verifiedByTest":true},"fixedBehavior":{"output":"Returns [{\\"id\\": 1, \\"title\\": \\"Shopping List\\", ...}] — case-insensitive match works.","type":"success","verifiedByTest":true},"regressionTestId":"notes-app/search-case-sensitive"}},{"id":19,"title":"list_notes returns live objects — mutations affect the internal store","state":"open","labels":["bug","good first issue"],"opened_by":"infra-nerd-9","opened_at":"2026-08-28","assigned_to":null,"body":"Found a subtle bug. `list_notes()` returns the actual internal note dicts, not copies. So if you do this:\\n\\n```python\\nnotes = list_notes()\\nnotes[0][\'title\'] = \'overwritten\'\\n```\\n\\n...the internal store is now corrupted. The note with id 1 permanently has the wrong title.\\n\\nThis is a encapsulation issue. The function should return copies of the notes, not references to the internal objects.\\n\\nI know it\'s labeled good first issue but this touches how the storage layer works. You need to understand the difference between shallow and deep copies in Python before fixing it, and consider whether a shallow copy is sufficient here or whether a deep copy is needed.","comments":[{"author":"tariq-dev","date":"2026-08-29","body":"Good report. The body field is just a string so a shallow copy via `dict(note)` is enough for now. If we add nested objects later we\'d need `copy.deepcopy`. Worth noting that in the PR."},{"author":"curious-dev","date":"2026-09-01","body":"What\'s the difference between shallow and deep copy here?"},{"author":"infra-nerd-9","date":"2026-09-01","body":"Shallow copy creates a new dict but keeps references to the same nested objects. Deep copy recursively copies everything. For now title and body are strings (immutable), so shallow is fine."},{"author":"tariq-dev","date":"2026-09-02","body":"Correct. Whoever picks this up — please add a test that confirms mutating the returned list doesn\'t affect the stored note."}],"lesson":"good_first_issue_hard","lesson_feedback":"Labeled good first issue, but the maintainer\'s own comments explain this needs understanding of shallow vs deep copy and the storage layer\'s design. The fix is technically one line, but you shouldn\'t write it without understanding why. If you\'re not sure about copy semantics in Python yet, this isn\'t the right starting point.","reproduction":{"issueId":"notes-app-19","buggyVersion":"1.0.0","fixedVersion":"1.1.0","reproductionSteps":[{"instruction":"Save a note: save_note(title=\'Original title\')"},{"instruction":"Call list_notes() and store the result."},{"instruction":"Mutate the first note: notes[0][\'title\'] = \'Corrupted\'"},{"instruction":"Call list_notes() again and check the title."}],"expectedResult":"The second list_notes() call still returns \'Original title\'","buggyBehavior":{"output":"Second list_notes() returns \'Corrupted\' — internal state was silently modified.","type":"silent_failure","verifiedByTest":true},"fixedBehavior":{"output":"Second list_notes() still returns \'Original title\' — internal state is protected.","type":"success","verifiedByTest":true},"regressionTestId":"notes-app/mutable-list"}},{"id":23,"title":"Notes disappear sometimes when I add a lot of them","state":"open","labels":["bug"],"opened_by":"frustrated-user-77","opened_at":"2026-09-05","assigned_to":null,"body":"Hey, I\'ve been using this for a bit and sometimes notes just disappear. I\'ll add like 10 notes and then when I list them some are missing. Not sure what\'s happening.\\n\\nCan someone help?","comments":[{"author":"tariq-dev","date":"2026-09-06","body":"Hi, thanks for the report. Could you share the exact steps to reproduce this? Specifically: which version are you on, how are you calling save_note, and what does list_notes return when this happens? Logs or a code snippet would help."},{"author":"frustrated-user-77","date":"2026-09-06","body":"I\'ll try to reproduce it again and send more info."}],"lesson":"no_reproduction_steps","lesson_feedback":"No steps to reproduce, no version, no code snippet or logs. The maintainer already asked for more info and the reporter hasn\'t followed up. You can\'t fix what you can\'t reproduce. The right move here is to ask for the missing info, not to start guessing at a fix.","reproduction":{"issueId":"notes-app-23","buggyVersion":null,"fixedVersion":null,"reproductionSteps":[{"instruction":"Read the issue body carefully."},{"instruction":"Check if there are steps to reproduce."},{"instruction":"Check if a version is mentioned."},{"instruction":"Check if there\'s a code snippet or error log."}],"expectedResult":"A reproducible bug report with steps, version, and actual vs expected output.","buggyBehavior":{"output":"Not enough information to reproduce. No steps, no version, no error message provided.","type":"wrong_output","verifiedByTest":false},"fixedBehavior":{"output":"N/A — issue cannot be investigated without more information from the reporter.","type":"success","verifiedByTest":false},"regressionTestId":null}}]')},68218,(a,b,c)=>{b.exports=[{id:2,title:"KeyError when looking up a city that can't be found",kind:"bug",state:"open",labels:["bug"],opened_by:"preethi-codes",opened_at:"2025-04-22",assigned_to:null,body:"If you pass a city name that the API doesn't recognise, the whole program crashes with a KeyError instead of showing an error message.\n\n```\nKeyError: 'main'\n```\n\nI mistyped the city name and got this instead of a helpful message.\n\n**Steps to reproduce:**\n1. Call `parse_weather` with a response that has no `main` key (e.g. a 404 response from the API)\n2. Observe: unhandled KeyError, program exits\n\n**Expected:** A clean error message like \"City not found.\"\n**Actual:** Unhandled KeyError\n\n**Version:** 1.0.0\n**Python:** 3.12",comments:[{author:"meera-writes-code",date:"2025-04-24",body:"Reproduced this too. Happens with any city name that returns a non-200 response."}],lesson:"fix_pending_unmerged",lesson_feedback:"This bug is real and reproducible. But before you start: check the open PRs. Someone has already submitted a fix. It hasn't been merged — not because it's wrong, but because the maintainer hasn't responded to anything in over a year.",reproduction:{issueId:"weatherly-2",buggyVersion:"1.0.0",fixedVersion:"1.1.0",reproductionSteps:[{instruction:"Open weather.py and find the parse_weather function."},{instruction:"Call parse_weather({'cod': '404', 'message': 'city not found'})"},{instruction:"Observe what happens."}],expectedResult:"parse_weather returns an error dict instead of crashing.",buggyBehavior:{output:'Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File "<stdin>", line 1, in <module>\n  File "src/weather.py", line 9, in parse_weather\n    temp = data["main"]["temp"]\nKeyError: \'main\'',type:"crash",verifiedByTest:!0},fixedBehavior:{output:"{'error': 'Unexpected API response: missing weather data.'}",type:"validation_error",verifiedByTest:!0},regressionTestId:"weatherly/missing-main-key"}},{id:5,title:"Add option to display temperature in Fahrenheit",kind:"feature",state:"open",labels:["good first issue"],opened_by:"kiran-weather-dev",opened_at:"2025-05-03",assigned_to:null,body:"It would be useful to support a `--unit` flag so users can choose Celsius or Fahrenheit. The conversion is straightforward: `F = C * 9/5 + 32`.\n\nThis would be a good first contribution if anyone wants to pick it up.",comments:[{author:"curious-dev",date:"2026-05-04",body:"Is anyone working on this? I'd be interested in picking it up."}],lesson:"good_first_issue_hard",lesson_feedback:"The change itself is small, but consider the context before starting. Check the last commit date, the open PRs, and whether the maintainer has replied to anything recently.",reproduction:null},{id:7,title:"IndexError when API returns empty weather list",kind:"bug",state:"open",labels:["bug"],opened_by:"infra-nerd-9",opened_at:"2025-05-05",assigned_to:null,body:"Another crash in `parse_weather`. If the API returns a response where the `weather` array is empty, line `data['weather'][0]` raises an IndexError.\n\n```\nIndexError: list index out of range\n```\n\n**Steps to reproduce:**\n1. Call `parse_weather` with `{'main': {'temp': 18.0, 'humidity': 55}, 'weather': []}`\n2. Observe: IndexError\n\n**Expected:** A clean error message.\n**Actual:** Unhandled IndexError\n\n**Version:** 1.0.0\n**Python:** 3.12",comments:[{author:"anon-contributor",date:"2025-11-20",body:"I'll take this one."},{author:"random-dev-42",date:"2026-01-15",body:"Any progress on this? Still hitting the crash."}],lesson:"claimed_never_started",lesson_feedback:"Someone claimed this over 10 months ago. No PR was ever opened. Before you start, leave a comment asking if they're still working on it — and also check the overall repo health before investing time here.",reproduction:{issueId:"weatherly-7",buggyVersion:"1.0.0",fixedVersion:"1.1.0",reproductionSteps:[{instruction:"Open weather.py and find the parse_weather function."},{instruction:"Call parse_weather({'main': {'temp': 18.0, 'humidity': 55}, 'weather': []})"},{instruction:"Observe what happens."}],expectedResult:"parse_weather returns an error dict instead of crashing.",buggyBehavior:{output:'Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File "<stdin>", line 1, in <module>\n  File "src/weather.py", line 11, in parse_weather\n    description = data["weather"][0]["description"]\nIndexError: list index out of range',type:"crash",verifiedByTest:!0},fixedBehavior:{output:"{'error': 'Unexpected API response: weather list is empty.'}",type:"validation_error",verifiedByTest:!0},regressionTestId:"weatherly/empty-weather-list"}},{id:20,title:"API key invalid?",kind:"question",state:"open",labels:[],opened_by:"frustrated-user-77",opened_at:"2026-02-28",assigned_to:null,body:"Getting an error about invalid API key. Not sure what's happening. Can someone help?",comments:[],lesson:"no_reproduction_steps",lesson_feedback:"No steps, no error message, no Python version, no indication of where they got their API key. There is not enough information here to help. The right move is to ask for the missing details — but also check whether the maintainer is likely to respond at all.",reproduction:null},{id:22,title:"Is this project still maintained?",kind:"question",state:"open",labels:[],opened_by:"random-dev-42",opened_at:"2026-07-20",assigned_to:null,body:"The last commit was over a year ago and open PRs are going unanswered. Is anyone still maintaining this? I'd like to contribute but I'm not sure it's worth the effort if nothing gets reviewed.",comments:[{author:"anon-contributor",date:"2026-07-22",body:"I opened a PR 7 months ago and never heard back. I think it's safe to say it's abandoned."},{author:"meera-writes-code",date:"2026-08-01",body:"Same experience here. Moving on to other projects."}],lesson:"health_signal",lesson_feedback:"This is the clearest signal in the repo. Last commit 13+ months ago. Four open PRs, all unanswered. No maintainer reply anywhere. This is what an abandoned repo looks like. The right call is to walk away.",reproduction:null}]}];

